2. KEY TOPICS DISCUSSED

2.1 Flattening Procedure

• Accepts parameters: source DB/schema/table, target DB/schema, variant columns, variant keys, foreign key path, relational columns, metadata flag, sync mode, and recursive flag
• Staging table holds raw JSON in 'data' column. Variant keys define flattening depth
• Foreign key (quotation number/claim number) propagated from parent to all child tables for use in upsert Procedure
• Supports both Full Refresh Overwrite (FRO) and Incremental Update (INU) modes via sync mode parameter
  - FRO creates permanent tables
  - INU creates temporary tables for later upsert into permanent tables
• Safety checks: if base or child table is deleted, procedure recreates it from staging table (for base table) or parent-level table (for child table)
• Metadata procedure retrieves column metadata (level, key, path, data type) used to build DDL statements dynamically
• Dictionaries/maps built for level-to-key, alias, and path mapping to help in DDL construction and recursively flattening json
• FROM query generated dynamically: arrays require FLATTEN; plain do not
• Audit columns (file name, refresh date, created-by,..) excluded from hash record to avoid false upserts
• Procedure calls itself recursively for child table levels. Output table names passed to Upsert Procedure

2.2 Upsert Procedure
• Parameters: source DB/schema/table, target DB/schema/table, and foreign key
• Validates both source and target tables exist and column lists match before proceeding
• Transaction initiated for atomicity (ACID compliance); any failure triggers ull rollback
• All foreign keys present in incremental data are deleted from permanent table, then latest records re-inserted — effectively a delete-and-insert (upsert) operation

2.3 Mirror Result Logging
• After each pipeline run, Mirror Result generated capturing:
  - Base table check status
  - Metadata differences (new columns)
  - DDL queries executed
  - Upsert results per table
• XCOM results are temporary (not stored permanently) and sent via email after each DAG run
• Duplicate report checks stage table for duplicate JSON files using foreign key, file name, and timestamp. Sensitive data masked, error-code JSON data unmasked for debugging
• Email summary includes: total records, distinct records, min/max file timestamp of that load, Mirror Result, and duplicate json report
• One email sent per topic per DAG run. Multiple runs per day means multiple emails

2.4 Recon DAG
• Separate Recon DAG runs after ingestion and executes SQL minus queries comparing Claim Register and Premium Register against Maximus Raw base tables
• Outputs mismatch report showing total JSONs in S3, total mismatches, and Excel sheet for review

3. Q&A SUMMARY

Q: Will we receive multiple emails if pipeline runs more frequently than daily?
A: Yes – one email per topic per DAG run. Multiple runs = multiple emails.
Q: How to rerun a specific table for a specific date?
A: Trigger the Orchestrator DAG manually with the table name. For backdated 
   reprocessing, clear json records upto that specific date and update the last_modified_timestamp
   from state table and trigger the Orchestrator dag with that table name.